  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "sámi-music-tunes-forms-history-and-recordings",
      "title": "Sámi Music — Tunes — Forms, History, and Recordings",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** Nordic Folk | **Region:** nordic-sami | **Tier:** 4.5\n**Prerequisites:** regions-nordic-sami-07-representative-depth\n**Tags:** nordic, sami, nordic, tunes, —"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "joik listening studies\n\nSámi Music belongs to the **Nordic Folk** curriculum. Mari Boine\nWimme\n\nThis lesson develops listening precision for **joik listening studies** — connecting repertoire, instruments, dance, and the institutions that transmit music today.\n\n![Traditional musicians](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fiddle.jpg?width=640)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "compared-with-norway",
      "title": "Compared with Norway",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Norwegian Folk Music** (`regions-nordic-norway-08-tunes`) covers the same lesson slot from the anchor perspective. This lesson stresses: **joik listening studies**.\n\n| Topic | Norway anchor | Sámi Music (this lesson) |\n|-------|------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| Focus | Fanitullen, Springar forms… | joik listening studies… |\n\n**Parallel anchor lesson:** `regions-nordic-norway-08-tunes`\n**Secondary comparison (Ireland):** `regions-celtic-ireland-08-tunes`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "core-material",
      "title": "Core material",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "joik listening studies\n\nStudy **sámi music** through listening first. Wikipedia's article on [Sámi music](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sámi_music) orients geography and history; recordings prove how musicians phrase ornaments, tempo, and ensemble balance in real rooms."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tune-studies",
      "title": "Tune studies",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Representative repertoire includes **joik listening studies**. Use the lesson tune panel and **Play all** to compare multiple settings of the same title or form."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "regional-accent-and-social-context",
      "title": "Regional accent and social context",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Tradition lives in **community practice** — festivals, dances, teaching networks, and archives — not only on concert stages. For **Nordic Folk**, ask who leads (voice, fiddle, pipes, percussion), whether dance drives repertoire, and how revival institutions shaped what you hear on modern recordings."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "instruments-and-voices-in-this-lesson",
      "title": "Instruments and voices in this lesson",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Lead voices: **joik, fiddle**. Ensemble layer: **contemporary ensemble**. Compare timbre and role with the anchor unit before assuming techniques transfer unchanged."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "forms-dance-and-rhythm",
      "title": "Forms, dance, and rhythm",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Named forms include **joik types**. Dance context: **ritual and festival**. Metre and foot-tap feel are not decorative — they explain why musicians accent certain beats and phrase repeats."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "history-transmission-and-today",
      "title": "History, transmission, and today",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Historical threads: **rights movement, revival**. Contemporary scenes: **Riddu Riđđu**. Tune repertoire anchor: **joik listening studies**. Follow lesson playlists and reading lists for recorded examples."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "listening-habits",
      "title": "Listening habits",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "1. Queue one field or archive recording and one contemporary ensemble track.\n2. Log metre, lead instrument, ornament, and social context in four columns.\n3. Return to the parallel anchor lesson if comparison feels abstract.\n4. Avoid generic world-music compilations when building ear training."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "study-facts",
      "title": "Study facts",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- joik listening studies\n- Lead instruments: joik, fiddle\n- Ensemble: contemporary ensemble\n- Forms: joik types\n- Dance: ritual and festival\n- History: rights movement, revival\n- Scenes: Riddu Riđđu\n- Repertoire: joik listening studies\n- Compared with Norway: regional accent and social context differ.\n- Revival movements and media shaped twentieth-century practice.\n- Dance rhythm and tune metre are linked in this tradition."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "key-points",
      "title": "Key points",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- Sámi Music is a living tradition with regional accent.\n- Focus: joik listening studies.\n- Compared with Norway: shared heritage, distinct emphasis.\n- Forms: joik types.\n- Dance: ritual and festival.\n- Transmission: rights movement, revival."
        }
      ]
    },
